# Aarogya Edge AI Rules

## AI Purpose

Aarogya uses AI for:

• Personalized baseline deviation
• Health risk detection
• Physiological deterioration detection
• Fall detection
• Disaster-context risk assessment

The AI must not be presented as a general disease diagnosis system.

## Personal Baseline

The personal baseline is a first class component.

The system should learn relevant normal patterns for the individual.

Potential baseline variables:

• Resting HR
• Typical SpO₂
• Typical activity
• Typical sleep
• Temperature where available
• Typical movement patterns

Do not assume that a single fixed threshold defines risk for every user.

## Baseline Calculation

Baseline logic must be:

• Deterministic where possible
• Explainable
• Testable
• Versioned

Do not make baseline behavior hidden inside a black box unnecessarily.

## Sensor Fusion

Where appropriate, combine:

Physiological data
+
Behavioral data
+
Environmental data
+
Personal baseline
+
Disaster context

Example:

High HR alone may indicate exercise.

High HR
+
low movement
+
elevated temperature
+
high humidity
+
large deviation from baseline

can indicate elevated risk.

Do not claim that this combination proves a medical condition.

## Risk Levels

Use explicit risk levels:

LOW
MODERATE
HIGH
CRITICAL

Risk level must have documented interpretation.

Avoid arbitrary thresholds without documentation.

## Explainability

Where technically possible, the application should provide reasons for a risk state.

Example:

"Heart rate is significantly above your usual baseline."

"Prolonged physical activity detected."

"SpO₂ has decreased from your recent baseline."

Do not expose raw model mathematics to normal users unless useful.

## Fall Detection

A fall detector should consider a sequence rather than a single acceleration spike.

Potential sequence:

Acceleration spike
→ impact
→ orientation change
→ inactivity

The system should minimize false positives from:

• Jumping
• Dropping the phone
• Running
• Sitting quickly
• Normal exercise

A possible fall should trigger confirmation before declaring a critical event when circumstances permit.

## Physiological Deterioration

The system should evaluate changes over time.

Do not rely only on a single sensor reading.

Use time windows where appropriate.

Example:

60 second window

5 minute trend

Recent baseline deviation

The exact windows must be validated through testing.

## Missing Data

Sensors can fail.

If SpO₂ is unavailable:

Do not assume SpO₂ = 0.

Do not invent a reading.

Mark it unavailable.

Risk logic must understand missing data.

## Model Metadata

Every deployed model should have:

• Name
• Version
• Input schema
• Output schema
• Preprocessing version
• Training dataset identifier
• Evaluation results
• Target runtime

## Model Performance

Measure:

• Inference latency
• Memory usage
• Model size
• CPU usage where practical
• Battery impact where practical

Do not invent these numbers.

## Edge Deployment

Models should run locally whenever the feature is classified as critical.

Preferred deployment technologies:

• ONNX Runtime Mobile
• TensorFlow Lite

Final technology should be chosen based on actual model requirements.

## Synthetic Data

Synthetic/replayed sensor data is acceptable during prototype development.

It must be labeled clearly as simulation data.

Synthetic data must not be presented as clinical evidence.

Research datasets and their allowed uses are defined in DATASETS.md.

Never silently mix synthetic replay data with real physiological recordings.

## No Fake AI

Do not create a UI that claims AI detected something when the model did not.

For a demo:

Simulated sensor stream
→ actual preprocessing
→ actual inference
→ actual risk engine
→ actual application state

The simulation should exercise the real pipeline.

## Clinical Claims

Never invent:

• Diagnostic accuracy
• Sensitivity
• Specificity
• Clinical validation
• Patient outcomes

unless supported by actual testing and appropriate evidence.
